   \]

2. **Determine the moles of oxygen:**
   \[
   \text{Moles of oxygen} = \frac{\text{Mass of oxygen}}{\text{Molar mass of oxygen}} = \frac{57.5 \text{ g}}{32 \text{ g/mol}} = 1.8 \text{ moles}
   \]

3. **Determine the moles of sulphur required:**
   The reaction between sulphur and oxygen is:
   \[
   \text{S} + \text{O}_2 \rightarrow \text{SO}_2
   \]
   From the balanced equation, 1 mole of sulphur reacts with 1 mole of oxygen. Thus, the moles of sulphur required are equal to the moles of oxygen:
   \[
   \text{Moles of sulphur} = 1.8 \text{ moles}
   \]

4. **Calculate the mass of sulphur required:**
   \[
   \text{Mass of sulphur} = \text{Moles of sulphur} \times \text{Molar mass of sulphur} = 1.8 \text{ moles} \times 32 \text{ g/mol} = 57.6 \text{ g}
   \]

5. **Calculate the mass of SO₂ produced:**
   \[
   \text{Mass of SO}_2 = \text{Moles of sulphur} \times \text{Molar mass of SO}_2 = 1.8 \text{ moles} \times 64 \text{ g/mol} = 115.2 \text{ g}
   \]

6. **Calculate the weight of residual gases:**
