- \( \frac{2\pi}{3} \)

The LCM of \( 1 \) and \( 3 \) is \( 3 \), so the LCM of \( \pi \) and \( \frac{2\pi}{3} \) is \( 3\pi \). Therefore, the period of \( f(x) \) is \( \boxed{3\pi} \).

**Amplitude of \( f(x) \):**
The amplitude of \( f(x) \) is not simply the sum of the amplitudes of the individual terms. The superposition of the two sinusoidal functions results in a more complex waveform. The amplitude of \( f(x) \) is the maximum difference between the maximum and minimum values of \( f(x) \). However, without further analysis (e.g., using the principle of superposition or plotting the function), it is not straightforward to determine the exact amplitude.

