<start_of_turn>model
","(a) The acceleration of the proton is given by the force on the proton divided by its mass. The force on the proton is given by the product of the charge and the electric field:  
\[
F = qE = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}.
\]  
The acceleration is then:  
\[
a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2.
\]  
The magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2}\).

(b) Using the kinematic equation for velocity:  
\[
v = u + at,
\]  
where \(u = 0 \, \text{m/s}\) (since the proton starts from rest), \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\), and \(a = 4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2\), we solve for \(t\):  
\[
t = \frac{v - u}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s} - 0 \, \text{m/s}}{4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2} = 7.25 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{s}.
\]  
