To calculate the work done, we use the formula:

\[
W = F \cdot d \cdot \cos \theta
\]

Where:
- \( W = \text{work done} \)
- \( F = \text{force applied} = 60.0 \, \text{N} \)
- \( d = \text{displacement} = 10.0 \, \text{m} \)
- \( \theta = \text{angle of the force relative to the displacement} = 60.0° \)

Substituting the values:

\[
W = 60.0 \, \text{N} \cdot 10.0 \, \text{m} \cdot \cos 60.0°
\]

\[
W = 600 \, \text{N m} \cdot 0.5
\]

\[
W = 300 \, \text{J}
\]

Thus, the work done is \(\boxed{300 \, \text{J}}\).
