","The maximum deflection \( \sigma_{\text{max}} \) of a triangular-shaped cantilever beam under a concentrated load \( P \) at the free end is given by:
\[
\sigma_{\text{max}} = \frac{P L^3}{6 E t^2}
\]
This formula is derived from the principle of energy methods. The strain energy stored in the beam is equal to the work done by the applied load. The strain energy is:
\[
U = \frac{1}{2} \sum_{i=1}^{N} t_i w_i \sigma_i^2
\]
For a triangular beam, the stress varies linearly with the height, so the stress at a distance \( x \) from the fixed end is:
\[
\sigma = \frac{P x}{b x} = \frac{P}{b}
\]
The strain energy is:
\[
U = \frac{1}{2} \int_0^L t \frac{y}{L} \left( \frac{P}{b} \right)^2 dx = \frac{t P^2}{2 b^2 L} \int_0^L x^2 dx = \frac{t P^2}{2 b^2 L} \left( \frac{L^3}{3} \right) = \frac{t P^2 L^2}{6 b^2}
\]
The work done by the applied load is:
\[
W = P \times \delta
\]
Equating the strain energy and work done:
\[
\frac{t P^2 L^2}{6 b^2} = P \times \delta \implies \delta = \frac{t L^2}{6 b^2}
\]
Thus, the maximum deflection is:
\[
\delta = \frac{P L^3}{6 E t^2}
\]
The maximum deflection \( \delta \) of a triangular-shaped cantilever beam under a concentrated load \( P \) at the free end is:
\[
\boxed{\delta = \frac{P L^3}{6 E t^2}}
