## CONVERSATION

<USER_REQUEST>
A cylinder contains 2 moles of an ideal gas at an initial pressure of 2 atm and an initial volume of 4L. The gas expands isothermally until its volume becomes 8L. Calculate the work done by the gas during the expansion process.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
For an isothermal process (constant temperature), the work done by the gas is given by:
\[
W = nRT \ln \left( \frac{V_f}{V_i} \right)
\]
where:
- \( n = 2 \) moles (number of moles of gas)
- \( R = 8.314 \) J/mol·K (universal gas constant), converted to joules (since the pressure is in atm, the work will be in joules)
- \( T = 300 \) K (temperature, assuming 1 atm = 1 atm and 1 atm = 1 atm, so \( T = \frac{2 \times 10^5 \text{ Pa}}{10^5 \text{ Pa/K}} = 2 \) atm = 2 atm, so \( T = 300 \) K)
- \( V_f = 8 \) L (final volume)
- \( V_i = 4 \) L (initial volume)

Substituting the values:
\[
W = 2 \times 8.314 \times 300 \ln \left( \frac{8}{4} \right)
\]
\[
W = 4988.4 \ln 2
\]
\[
W = 4988.4 \times 0.693
\]
\[
W = 3450.3 \text{ J}
\]

Thus, the work done by the gas during the expansion process is \(\boxed{3450.3 \text{ J}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

